            {
                "node": {
                    "created_at": "1775462097",
                    "text": "This viral exchange perfectly captures the age-old debate regarding how early exposure to specific operating systems shapes our cognitive development and technical troubleshooting abilities. The original poster, Annie, suggests a correlation study between children raised on Mac versus Windows computers to see if one leads to higher tech illiteracy or weaker problem-solving skills later in life. The underlying theory is that \"walled garden\" ecosystems might stifle the curiosity required to understand how systems actually function. However, the thread takes a hilarious and sharp turn when a user mentions installing Linux at age twelve—a feat of digital endurance that usually involves hours of troubleshooting and terminal commands. Annie’s quick-witted reply about excluding certain demographics from the study to avoid skewing the data highlights a popular internet trope: the \"tech wizard\" archetype. It’s a witty commentary on how we learn to navigate the digital world and the different paths to computer literacy.\n#TechHumor #LinuxUser #MacVsWindows #ComputerScience #InternetCulture",
                    "id": "18088652581977093"
                }
            }
        ]
    },
